  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q12-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Find all pairs of integers $(x,y)$ such that\n$$x^3-y^3=1729.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(x,y)=(1,-12),(9,-10),(10,-9),(12,-1)$.\n\nFactor the difference of cubes and test the possible positive values of $x-y$.",
      "worked": "Since $x^3>y^3$, let $d=x-y>0$ and $s=x+y$. Factorising gives\n$$\\begin{align*}1729&=(x-y)(x^2+xy+y^2)\\\\1729&=d\\left(\\frac{3s^2+d^2}{4}\\right).\\end{align*}$$\nThus $d$ divides $1729=7\\times13\\times19$. Also $d^3\\leq4\\times1729=6916$, so $d\\leq19$. The possible divisors are $1,7,13,19$, and\n$$s^2=\\frac{6916/d-d^2}{3}.$$\nFor $d=1,7,13,19$, respectively, this gives $s^2=2305,313,121,1$. The first two are not squares. Therefore $(d,s)=(13,\\pm11)$ or $(19,\\pm1)$. Using\n$$x=\\frac{s+d}{2},\\qquad y=\\frac{s-d}{2},$$\nwe obtain $(x,y)=(1,-12),(9,-10),(10,-9),(12,-1)$. Each pair satisfies the equation, and the divisor bound excludes all other pairs.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 95
  },
